d #edb/ref 17592186558371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ac28018-b80f-5b5b-b911-ee1b1c7f7b2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-88494" :question/problem "Determine which of the following is the graph of $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{y}+8}{{y}^{3/2}}\\,$ by completing the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |" :question/worked-solution "To graph this curve, we can start by calculating the value of $x$ for various values of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1 & \\,⟶\\,x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{1}+8}{{1}^{3/2}}=10 \\\\ y=4 & \\,⟶\\,x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}+8}{{4}^{3/2}}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=9 & \\,⟶\\,x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{9}+8}{{9}^{3/2}}=\\frac{14}{27} \\\\ y=16 & \\,⟶\\,x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{16}+8}{{16}^{3/2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize these results in the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $10$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{14}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n\n$$\n(10,1),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},4),\\,(\\frac{14}{27},9),\\,(\\frac{1}{4},16).\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points and connect them with a smooth curve, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/56/56b5c0ad101bb2d26a66fa84accdd11c629f150f64892aad4c20194d647428e8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2981f6-bfbf-5c1e-a4a2-2814dc24804a" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Quadratic Function Where X Is a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232f4778-6f65-5e2c-b37b-3359f57ef8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/ca9ec14867ac40daa432e2a225a189bb3f3dcd0179f074702c83405a5db9436d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557941} {:answer/id #uuid "54d9d7fd-9e5a-5c64-8c19-8b856d6fd9de" :answer/ty